> Sovereign credit ratings
> Sovereign creditworthiness is increasingly shaped by the impacts of climate change. A sovereign’s credit rating influences its cost of borrowing, with direct implications for both public and private financing. Evidence shows that physical climate risks are already translating into sovereign downgrades and higher borrowing costs, particularly for emerging and developing economies, while the benefits of adaptation remain under-recognized (Volz et al., 2020; Zenios, 2022).
> 
> ​​Empirical research demonstrates that countries exposed to climate shocks face higher risk premia and increased default probabilities. For example, the 2022 floods in Pakistan affected 33 million people, caused USD 30 billion in damages, and led Fitch to downgrade the country’s credit rating, citing liquidity pressures exacerbated by the disaster (Fitch, 2022). Forward-looking models suggest that by 2030, nearly 60 countries could face average sovereign downgrades of 0.7 notches, rising to over 2 notches by 2100 (Klusak et al., 2023).
> 
> Recent downgrade data illustrates this trajectory. We apply an existing analysis to evaluate these impacts and find that all regions of the world are at risk of downgrades (Klusak et al. 2023). In contrast to our macroeconomic results, we also find that the greatest impacts of climate change on sovereign credit ratings fall on upper middle income and high-income countries, across all regions. This is likely driven by the greater potential reductions in credit ratings for higher income countries. Some highly vulnerable countries do not have a credit rating.
> 
> At the same time, the risk to credit worthiness for poorer countries is already present, with climate-vulnerable countries facing a measurable risk premium in their debt markets, which reflects both their heightened exposure to extreme events and their limited fiscal capacity to respond (Kling et al., 2018; Cevik & Jalles, 2021). 
> 
> Sovereign credit downgrading across regions and income groups in 2050
> 
> Impact of climate change on sovereign credit ratings, evaluated under a high-emissions scenario by Klusak et al. (2023), and adjusted to represent 2050. Number labels report the number of countries available in each region-income group from Klusak et al.
> 
> 
> Despite these risks, adaptation efforts are rarely reflected in credit assessments. Large-scale investments in resilient infrastructure, early warning systems, and disaster risk finance can materially reduce fiscal vulnerability. However, credit rating agencies typically acknowledge such measures only when they yield immediate fiscal benefits (Andersen et al., 2025). This results in systematic undervaluation of adaptation, even though empirical work shows that adaptive capacity is a significant determinant of sovereign credit outcomes (De Moor et al., 2018; Cevik & Jalles, 2022). The downgrades illustrated in Figures 1 and 2 highlight what is at stake: without adaptation, countries in climate-vulnerable regions face mounting fiscal stress and escalating borrowing costs.
> 
> The mispricing of climate risk and adaptation creates a vicious cycle. Vulnerable economies are penalized with higher borrowing costs, which constrain their ability to finance adaptation, leaving them more exposed to the next shock. As Figures 1 and 2 demonstrate, this cycle disproportionately affects low- and middle-income countries. Breaking it requires policy and market reforms that recognize adaptation as a credit-enhancing factor. Priority measures include improved disclosure and integration of climate risks into debt sustainability analyses, the development of innovative financial instruments such as resilience bonds and contingent credit lines, and stronger support from multilateral development banks (ADB, 2019; EBRD, 2019).
> 
> Sovereign downgrades linked to climate impacts are no longer hypothetical—they are observable realities. Unless adaptation is systematically integrated into sovereign credit assessments, many vulnerable economies risk being trapped in a downward spiral of downgrades and rising debt costs. Embedding adaptation into financial analysis is therefore not only an environmental imperative but also a fiscal one (Klusak et al., 2023; Boston Consulting Group & GRP, 2022).
> Other fiscal impacts
> Government expenditure is expected to increase and government revenue is expected to decrease with climatic shocks. Nevertheless, changes in public debt are uncertain as climate change may downgrade countries' credit ratings. 
> 
> The effect of climate change on public expenditures shows a wide variation. While the Australian Government (2023) estimates an increase in public expenditures of 33% per degree warming for Australia, Leppänen et al. (2015) estimate a decrease of 1.5 to 2.6% per degree of warming for Russia.
>  
> At a global level, harsh droughts lead to a significant increase of 0.11 percentage points in the government expenditure-to-GDP ratio, and an increase in the occurrence of extreme hot days reduces the government revenue-to-GDP ratio by 0.14 percentage points (Akaypi et al., 2025). In Australia, the increased frequency and severity of natural disasters are expected to increase expenditure by 3 to 3.6 times over the following 40 years (Australian Government, 2023). In the US, the public expenditure increase caused by extreme hot days raises the social cost of carbon by 23-33% (Barrage, 2024). 
> 
> Nevertheless, evidence of the effect of climatic shocks on public debt is not concluding. Two econometric studies based on historical data find that debt does not increase significantly after climatic shocks. According to a study from Akaypi et al. (2025), extreme temperatures or droughts do not have a significant impact on public debt at a global level. The authors find this result hard to interpret and do not provide any explanation for this finding.  A study in the US found that public debt was reduced 10 years following hurricane exposure. The main reason is that interest rates increased by 1% after hurricane exposure, due to the fact that these natural disasters increased the default risk by 0.1 percentage points. As a result, public debt did not increase, but government expenditure was reduced (Jerch et al., 2023).These findings suggest that governments might address fiscal deficits by reducing expenditure rather than relying solely on increased borrowing.
> 
> Other studies have used modeling approaches to understand debt impacts. Gagliardi et al. (2022) perform a stylised analysis of the fiscal impact of natural disasters, finding increases between 0.7 pp and 4.5 pp across EU countries under a 1.5 °C scenario and from 0.9 to 5.2 pp under a 2 °C scenario. Lamperti et al. (2019) use an agent-based model to evaluate the effects of capital and labour damages and find that the Debt-to-GDP ratio increases by up to 94% on average when climate change driven impacts on both are considered.
> 
> Interest rates are expected to increase annual interest payments on sovereign debt by US$45-67 billion under the RCP2.6 scenario and US$135-203 billion under the RCP8.5 scenario. 
>  
> The metric of fiscal (primary) balance seems ripe for analysis: climate change is likely to both reduce revenues and increase expenditures for governments, and the effect on their combination may be more tractable than either individually. However, evidence of fiscal balance is very sparse. Within our review, two papers provided this outcome: a stylised analysis of fiscal balance for EU countries due to natural disasters (Gagliardi et al.) and an analysis of optimal fiscal rules for Pacific Islands (Nakatani 2021).
